From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 16:01:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(llama-cpp): a second termination signal leaves at once
 instead of calling exit() in the handler

The backend's signal handler answered a second SIGTERM/SIGINT with
fprintf() and exit(1). exit() runs the exit handlers and the static
destructors from inside the handler while main() is in the middle of the
clean-up the first signal started (the gRPC server's shutdown, the GPU
runtime's teardown), and it can block there: "Received second interrupt,
terminating immediately." is printed and the process stays, its GPU memory
still held, until LocalAI's SIGKILL at the end of its 15 s grace.

A backend gets two signals whenever something between LocalAI and it
forwards one: LocalAI stops a backend with SIGTERM to its process group
(go-processmanager), so a wrapper or supervisor in that group that passes
the signal to its child delivers a second one a millisecond later. Seen on
an Intel Arc Pro B70 (SYCL) behind such a wrapper: 3 of 14 unloads took
15.06 s instead of 0.11-0.21 s and ended with exitCode=-1; started
directly, one signal, 0 of 22.

The second signal now leaves through second_signal.h: write(2) and
std::_Exit(1), async-signal-safe calls only. The message is unchanged.
second_signal_test.cpp (standalone, picked up by
backend/cpp/run-unit-tests.sh and by ctest) forks a child whose exit
handler never returns: it is gone at once with status 1, and the same
child leaving through exit(1) is still there a second later.

llama.cpp's own server has the same handler (tools/server/server.cpp).

Assisted-by: Claude Code
